  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Source-tool tests publish OutlineId addresses and retain mapped NodeIds\n directly in dependencies. This required Domain method is unused in\n those flows; stable-path and filesystem-storage tests use other domains."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs` `mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]` is valid.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced citation on an explicit function with a body, and the explanation is in fenced commentary. That is the form the built-in allows for a per-function coverage exception.\n- **Substance:** The commentary says the source-tool tests publish `OutlineId` addresses and keep mapped `NodeId`s directly in dependencies, so the required `node_path` method is never called there. It also says the stable-path and filesystem-storage tests use other domains. That is a real reason, not just difficulty.\n- **Agreement with current source:** I searched the crate for callers of `node_path`.\n  - Outside test domains, the only callers are `ToolAgent::run` and `GraphSession` (`node` and `dispatch`) in `agent/tools.rs`, and `edge_fingerprint` in `graph.rs`.\n  - `edge_fingerprint` is reached through `dependencies_are_current` and `ValidationContext::prepare_edges`.\n  - The `project_sources.rs` tests drive `ProjectSources` and `ValidationContext::read_source` directly. They also drive `Validator::refresh_evidence` and `verify` with `ReadSource`, which records only `Dependency::Node` entries (the test `source_capable_validator_tracks_deterministic_reads_and_reuses_saved_results` asserts this) and never runs the tool agent. `Project::outgoing_edges` returns an empty list.\n  - The path-stability and filesystem-storage tests (`tests/contracts.rs`, `tests/storage.rs`) use their own domains.\n\nThe claims therefore match the code. The item references no requirement with `{impl}` or `{test}`, so nothing is redundant.",
